specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:1:# Tool Input Validation
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:8:Every tool registered in `src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts` declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` that is forwarded to the configured AI provider, and `ToolRegistry.execute` validates the model-supplied `input` against that schema before calling the tool's executor. When validation fails, `execute` returns a rejection `ToolResponse` describing what was wrong rather than throwing, so the orchestrator feeds the failure back to the model as a `tool_result` within the existing tool-round budget.
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:12:- When `input` omits a property listed in `required`, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the missing property as required and never invokes the executor ([validated by: returns a required-property rejection before invoking the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L4), [validated by: rejects input missing a required property with a ToolResponse and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L16)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:13:- When `input` supplies a property whose value does not match its declared JSON Schema type, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` naming the failing field path and expected type without invoking the executor, and neither `result` nor the emitted log record contains the offending property value ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property before invoking the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L56), [validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property naming the field and expected type without leaking the offending value to the result or logs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L46)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:14:- When `input` satisfies `required`, declared property types and `enum`, `ToolRegistry.execute` invokes the executor once and passes the input object through unchanged — including undeclared extra properties — without injecting `additionalProperties: false`, coercing types or applying defaults ([validated by: passes valid undeclared properties through to the executor unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L31)). When an enum fails, its rejection names the property and the allowed values ([validated by: rejects an enum value with the allowed values](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L83)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:17:## Schema compilation and caching
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:19:- A static `ToolDefinition` has its `inputSchema` compiled once at `register` time, so a schema that is not valid JSON Schema throws synchronously from `register` with an error message naming the offending tool ([validated by: throws at register time naming the tool when a static inputSchema cannot be compiled](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L94)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:20:- Registering an invalid static schema fails before the tool is stored, so callers receive an error that identifies the tool they attempted to register ([validated by: throws at register time naming the tool when a static inputSchema cannot be compiled](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L94)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:21:- A static tool's `inputSchema` is compiled at `register` time and reused on every `execute` call without re-reading or re-compiling `definition.inputSchema`.
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:22:- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` resolves its definition on each `execute` call and validates against the `inputSchema` returned for that call, caching compiled validators by schema object reference (`WeakMap`) so distinct returned schemas each enforce their own rules.
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:26:- Because a schema rejection returns a `ToolResponse` instead of throwing, `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts` sends the rejection `result` back to the provider as a `tool_result` message, allowing the model to issue a corrected tool call on the next round and complete the turn within `maxToolRounds` ([validated by: returns a schema rejection as a tool_result in the orchestrator loop so the model can correct the call on the next round](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L112)).
adrs/ADR-006-lint-suppressions.md:23:Five sites carry `eslint-disable-next-line` (or, in one case, a file-level `eslint-disable`) with a `--` reason referring here, under the four decisions below. Together they account for every finding these three rules report: run with `--no-inline-config` and the count is three `no-flag-params`, one `prefer-polymorphism` and one `test-imports-its-subject`. The sixth was withdrawn when its rule stopped firing - see the `validateMessage` entry.
adrs/ADR-006-lint-suppressions.md:31:**`re-lint/prefer-polymorphism` on `createProvider`** (`src/providers/providerFactory.ts`). The five-way `switch` on `config.type` narrows a discriminated union onto five factories that take five different config types. A lookup table keyed by the string loses that narrowing: TypeScript cannot prove the selected arm's parameter matches the value being dispatched, so the table needs a cast at exactly the point the union was buying safety. This is the same premise as the `validateMessage` entry below, and it is not the premise this entry carried before the ESM conversion. Until then each arm `require`d its provider's module lazily and the suppression was justified as a loading boundary - but `src/index.ts` statically re-exports all five factories, so the factory's laziness never prevented a single module load. The boundary that does work lives inside each provider, around the vendor SDK, and stays there.
adrs/ADR-006-lint-suppressions.md:33:**`re-lint/prefer-polymorphism` on `validateMessage`** (`src/transport/ws/validation.ts`). Withdrawn, not reversed. The `switch` on `message.type` narrows an untrusted WebSocket frame through a discriminated union, and a lookup keyed by string would lose that narrowing at precisely the point where the input is attacker-controlled and the type is a claim rather than a fact. The `default` arm rejecting unknown types is the security behaviour. But removing the `send_message` alias left the switch with two cases, below the rule's threshold, so the rule no longer fires and the directive became an unused-disable error. It is deleted. The reasoning above stands and the suppression should come back with it the moment a third case is added - which the add-a-message-type how-to walks a reader straight into.
